<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gao Cheng</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">521–549</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gao Cheng was the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-huan">Gao Huan</a> and regent of the Eastern Wei after his father's death in 547. He faced the defection of the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hou-jing">Hou Jing</a> to the south and continued the war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-wei">Western Wei</a>. He was assassinated by a kitchen slave in 549, and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wenxuan-of-northern-qi">Gao Yang</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-qi">Northern Qi</a> the following year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gao-cheng.jpg" alt="Gao Cheng" width="320" height="437" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E4%B8%96%E5%AD%90%E9%AB%98%E6%BE%84%EF%BC%88%E5%8C%97%E5%8F%B2%E6%BC%94%E7%BE%A9%EF%BC%89.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gao Cheng was born in 521, when his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-huan">Gao Huan</a> was still a courier between the Northern Wei capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> and the old capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/datong">Pingcheng</a>; his mother Lou Zhaojun came from a wealthy family. After Gao Huan defeated the Erzhu clan in 532, the 11-year-old boy received high titles as heir of his father's principality of Bohai. In 535 Gao Huan found that his son was having an affair with his concubine Zheng Dache, caned him 100 times and nearly replaced him as heir, until his friend Sima Ziru talked him round.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 536, aged 15, Gao Cheng took charge of the imperial government at Yecheng while his father kept his headquarters at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinyang">Jinyang</a>. He enforced the laws more harshly than his father and replaced promotion by seniority with the quick advancement of capable men. In 543 his assistant Cui Xian's quarrel with the general Gao Zhongmi, and Gao Cheng's attempt to rape Gao Zhongmi's wife Li Changyi, drove the general to surrender the key garrison of Hulao to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-wei">Western Wei</a>. In 544 Gao Cheng took over the duties of officials his father thought corrupt, and had Sima Ziru stripped of all offices.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-huan">Gao Huan</a> died in spring 547, and Gao Cheng kept the death secret at first. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hou-jing">Hou Jing</a> rebelled, Murong Shaozong defeated a Liang relief army at Hanshan late in 547, capturing Xiao Yuanming, and drove Hou to Liang by spring 548. After Emperor Xiaojing rebuked him, Gao Cheng had Cui Jishu punch the emperor three times; a plot against him was uncovered in winter 547, and the emperor was placed under arrest. In summer 549 Gao Cheng took Changshe from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-wei">Western Wei</a> general Wang Sizheng and recovered all the lands Hou had first taken.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In autumn 549, while discussing with Chen Yuankang, Cui Jishu and Yang Yin how to seize the throne, he was served a meal by Lan Jing, son of the Liang general Lan Qin, whom he had captured and refused to let be ransomed. Overhearing Gao Cheng say he had dreamed of the slave striking him and should kill him, Lan Jing came back and stabbed him to death. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-qi">Northern Qi</a> later honoured him as Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wenxiang">Wenxiang</a>, with the temple name Shizong.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gao Cheng” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Gao Huan's death in 547 his general Hou Jing defected to the south, and his son <b>Gao Cheng</b> was assassinated in 549.</blockquote><a href="/state/eastern-wei">Eastern Wei</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Jing County, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.69,116.26&amp;year=539" aria-label="Jing County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7453 808 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Gao Cheng"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7903 1078h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7903 1078h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.69,116.26&amp;year=539">What was here in 539 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
